<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The sync job. Rendered by install.sh; __PLACEHOLDERS__ are substituted there.

  StartInterval 900 plus RunAtLoad, because the icloudpd incremental pass IS the
  "anything new in iCloud" check: a quiet tick costs about a minute and no VM.
  launchd does not wake a sleeping Mac for it and collapses missed ticks into one
  at wake; the script is single-flight, so an overlapping tick logs one line and
  exits. RunAtLoad matters for a laptop that is shut down most nights, which a
  calendar tick would effectively never reach.

  IT RUNS THE APP BINARY, NOT THE SCRIPT. Listing a cloud-provider staging tree
  is permission-gated per app, and a bash that launchd spawned is its own
  responsible process: it gets "Operation not permitted" on every directory of
  the mount while a child of the app bundle lists it fine. `--sync` spawns the
  script as the app's child and waits. The wrapper waits for the app to exist so
  a fresh machine mid-install does not fail the job, but that wait is BOUNDED at
  five minutes and then exits 78 (EX_CONFIG): a job sleeping forever looks
  identical in `launchctl print` to a healthy idle one, and the whole design of
  this pipeline is that a thing which is not working says so. `exec` is fine
  because the FINAL image is the app.

  Background band with LowPriorityIO and Nice: calendar and interval jobs missed
  while the Mac slept all fire TOGETHER at wake, so priority, not scheduling, is
  what keeps this out of the way. stdout goes to /dev/null because the script
  owns its own rotated log; stderr is kept, and in normal operation it is empty.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>__LABEL__</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>app="__APP_BIN__"; n=0; while [ ! -x "$app" ]; do n=$((n+1)); [ "$n" -gt 60 ] &amp;&amp; { echo "$app never appeared - run build.sh" >&amp;2; exit 78; }; sleep 5; done; exec "$app" --sync</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>900</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
  <key>Nice</key><integer>5</integer>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/dev/null</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>__LOG_DIR__/sync.launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
